  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Express $\\frac{4-5x}{x(x-1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{4-5x}{x(x-1)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x$ |  | $x-1$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4-5x}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $x(x-1)$ gives\n\n$$\n4-5x=A(x-1)+Bx.\n$$\n\nFor $x=0$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-5(0) & =A(0-1)+B(0) \\\\ 4 & =-A \\\\ A & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-5(1) & =A(1-1)+B(1) \\\\ -1 & =B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4-5x}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{-4}{x}+\\frac{-1}{x-1}.\n$$",
